ps    :- list the process executing in the current terminal 

ps -e :- list all the proceess in the system 

ps -l :- list process information in long format  

ps -e | grep pts/1 : the info of ps -e temporarily stored in the pipe. And grep (global regular expression) will search for the particular pattern. grep will take information from the pipe and if the pattern is found display the entire line.

Process Control Block (PCB)
---------------------------

=> When a process is created, for every process manager will create the PCB
=> PCB contains information about a process in data structure manner

	Information like 

		* Process state
		* PID, PPID
		* Program counter 
		* CPU Register values 
		* CPU scheduling info 
		* Memory info 
		* Open files 


	Example : CPU Time Slicing and PCB

	Consider 3 programs 

		p1 : 200 lines of code 
		p2 : 100 lines of code 
		p3 : 400 lines of code 

		These programs are loaded into memory are converted into processes by the OS.
	
	CPU Scheduling Using Time Slicing

		Assume the CPU uses RR scheduling with a time slice of 10 milliseconds for each process 

		Execution Assumptions 

		In 10 ms :
		
			* p1 executes 50 lines 
			* p2 executes 30 lines 
			* p3 executes 40 lines 
	
	Execution Flow 

	1. p1 starts executing first. 

		p2 & p3 remain in the ready state, waiting for the CPU 
		After 10 ms, P1's time slice expires 

	2. Before p1 leaves the CPU 

		The current execution context of p1 is saved in its PCB
		This includes the last executed instruction and CPU state
	
	3. p2 gets the CPU next 

		Executes 30 lines within its time slice.
		Its context is saved into PCB (p2) when the time slice ends

	4. p3 then gets the CPU 

		Executes 40 lines.
		its context is saved into PCB (p3)

	5. This cycle repeats until all processes complete execution. 


 	PCB Structure (Concpetual View)
	

		[   Stack Pointer (SP)   ]
		[   Process ID (PID)     ]
		[   Parent PID (PPID)    ]
		[   Program Counter (PC) ]
		[   Memory Information   ]
		[   I/O Status           ]

	
	Context Switch (switching from one process to another process)
	--------------------------------------------------------------

		A context switch is the mechanism to store and restore the state or context of a process into PCB. So that a process execution can be resumed from the same point at a later time. 

	Using context switch multiple processes will share a single CPU 
	
	Context switch is mandatory for multiprocessing 

	For every process switch, there is a need of context switch 

	Due to context switch extra burdne on CPU, But without context switch acheving the mutliprocessing is not possible

	It is impossible to make context switch time to 0. But it is possible to minimize the context switch time using threads. 

	Life of PCB is life of the process. 




































































































	
